  "status": "SUCCESS",
  "summary": "All 483 tests pass (`python -m compileall app` clean).\n\n**1. Concurrent redline conflict (UC5)** — `SubmitReviewRequest` gained an optional `expectedVersion` (the frontend contract doesn't list it, so it's optional and absent means \"on the latest version\"; the name matches `EditClauseRequest.expectedVersion` already used here). In `submit_review`, a submission whose `expectedVersion` is behind `routing.current_version_number` is still written as its own negotiation version with its comment — nothing is overwritten, both versions are saved — but the reviewer is left `Pending`, the negotiation status does not advance, and the call commits and then returns **409** with \"A newer version was submitted while you were editing. Please refresh and reapply your changes.\" The reviewer can resubmit against the new version.\n\n**5. Mandatory clause can't be removed (UC3)** — `reject_clause` now refuses a clause with `is_mandatory` with **409** \"This clause is mandatory and cannot be removed from the contract.\" before touching status/version. Optional clauses reject exactly as before.\n\n**13. Audit log can't be changed (UC11)** — enforced twice in this repo: explicit PUT/PATCH/DELETE routes on `/api/v1/settings/audit-log/{entryId}` and `/api/v1/lifecycle/contracts/{contractId}/audit-log/{entryId}` that return **403** \"Audit log entries cannot be modified or deleted.\" (previously a bare 405 with no message), plus a new `app/models/immutable.py` guard registered on `TenantAuditLog` and `ClauseAuditLog` that blocks any UPDATE/DELETE at the persistence layer before SQLAlchemy emits it.\n\nReading taken: these three rules are backend enforcement of existing endpoints, so I changed only those code paths and left the other contract routes alone.\n\nThe database-level half of rule 13 (a Postgres trigger on `tenant_audit_log`/`clause_audit_log`) would need a new Alembic revision under `alembic/`, which is outside the paths this run may modify — so that is not done; the ORM-level guard is the enforcement below HTTP for now. The UI half of all three rules belongs to the frontend repository.",
  "files_changed": [
    "app/errors.py",
    "app/models/immutable.py",
    "app/models/lifecycle_clause.py",
